    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d27">

                <pb facs="0088" n="60" sameAs="frus:frus1945-50Intel/pg60"/>

                <head>27. Memorandum From Secretary of the Navy Forrestal to Secretary of State Byrnes<anchor n="0" xml:id="d27-f1"
                    /></head>
                <dateline><placeName>Washington</placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                        when="1945-10-13">October 13, 1945</date>.</dateline>
                <p>I am enclosing memoranda as follows:<list>
                        <label>1.</label>
                        <item>Captain Souers, Assistant
                            Chief of Naval Intelligence, to Mr. Eberstadt, who dealt with the question of central
                            intelligence in his report to me on the proposed unification of the
                            Armed Services;<anchor n="1" xml:id="d27-f2"/></item>
                        <label>2.</label>
                        <item>A memorandum from me to Admiral King;<anchor
                                n="2" xml:id="d27-f3"/></item>
                        <label>3.</label>
                        <item>A response from Admiral King.<anchor n="3"
                                xml:id="d27-f4"/></item>
                    </list></p>
                <p>I wish you would regard this as an informal transmission and return these papers
                    after reading. Subsequently, I would like to suggest<anchor n="4"
                        xml:id="d27-f5"/> that you put on the agenda of one of the meetings with
                        Bob Patterson and myself,<anchor n="5" xml:id="d27-f6"
                    /> this question, which it seems to me is one of the most important, considering
                    the state of the world, that we have before us as a government.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        
                            <hi rendition="#bold">James Forrestal</hi>
                        
                    </signed>
                </closer>





                <xi:include href="d27-a1.xml" xpointer="d27-a1"/>
                <xi:include href="d27-a2.xml" xpointer="d27-a2"/>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d27-f1" n="0" type="source" xml:id="d27-f1-content">
                <p>Source: National Archives and Records Administration, RG 59, Records of the
                    Department of State, Decimal File 1945–49, 101.5/10–1345. No classification
                    marking.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d27-f2" n="1" xml:id="d27-f2-content">
                <p>Printed as enclosure 1.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d27-f3" n="2" xml:id="d27-f3-content">
                <p>In this memorandum, October 4, Forrestal
                    proposed a meeting with Hoover, McCormack, Inglis, and Bissell.
                    (Ibid.) Attached to the same memorandum Forrestal apparently sent a copy of enclosure 1 and asked for
                    King’s “suggestions as to Navy objectives and recommended methods of
                    implementation.”</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d27-f4" n="3" xml:id="d27-f4-content">
                <p>Printed as enclosure 2.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d27-f5" n="4" xml:id="d27-f5-content">
                <p>At this point Forrestal added by hand in
                    the margin the words “for your consideration.”</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d27-f6" n="5" xml:id="d27-f6-content">
                <p>A reference to the periodic meetings of the Secretaries of State, War, and Navy
                    known as the “Committee of Three.”</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
